THE CROSS-CHECK HELD ON EVERY LEG: each R_ mutant left rldimi at 29/29, each D_ mutant left rlwimi at 28/28.

THE TWO MISSES, stated as what they are.
 * D_NO_MERGE.  The ROW-LEVEL prediction was right -- `alias rs=ra' FAIL, `full rot' ok -- and the score was
   not: table 2's three failures plus one new failure on 29 rows is 25, and the PRETABLE wrote 26.  The same slip
   as xscaleinten table 2's HEAD (a row counted on the wrong side of the arithmetic).
 * D_NOCIRC.  PRETABLE predicted `full rot' FAIL and labelled the leg UB ("record which rows, do not fit").  It
   PASSED, value exact (probe_rldimi_D_NOCIRC.raw).  That is MEASURED; the mechanism is NOT, and is offered as the
   likely one: with `m ++' unmasked, `1 << (63-m)' runs past bit 0 into undefined shifts, and if the host masks
   the count to six bits the loop keeps setting the same bits circularly until m wraps and meets its end value --
   a full mask, reached slowly, consistent with the census's near-timeout under this leg.  Either way the leg is
   killed by its seven other failures, as in table 2; `full rot' simply does not add to them on this build.

THE LITERAL PINS, CORRECTING TABLE 2's NOTE.  killtable2_20260912/POSTTABLE.out said `disasm total' and `census
run' "remain pins by construction, not by a measured kill".  The Opus pass-2 seat measured one:
../opus_pass2_20260912/rldimi_DM_NOAND_END.log (end test without `& 63': the SH=0 hang aborts the sessions) reads
`census run 0 / 495 FAIL' and `disasm total 8/16 FAIL' against the 27-row instrument.  Table 2's own legs produced
no short census; the pins do carry a measured kill.
